e opponent's range is uncertain; there are better hands to risk my stack on.\n**Answer:** -1\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation:\nThe current situation involves facing a massive all-in bet on the Flop (Round 1) after a checked Pre-flop round. I hold a 2 of Spades and 7 of Hearts on a board of 6d, 2c, Qd. This gives me a bottom pair of 2s with a weak 7 kicker. The opponent's all-in is a huge overbet relative to the small pot size (likely just the blinds). While this could be a bluff or a semi-bluff on a flush draw, it represents significant strength or polarization. Calling requires risking my entire stack of ~19,900 chips. Against a range of hands including top pair (Q), middle pair (6), overpairs, or better kickers, I am significantly behind. Even against a flush draw, I am barely ahead. The risk of losing a massive amount of chips (which hurts the cumulative score significantly) far outweighs the potential gain of a small pot with a weak hand.\n\nReason:\nWith only bottom pair and a weak kicker, calling a massive all-in overbet is extremely risky and mathematically unsound (negative Expected Value). It is better to preserve the chip stack for a better spot, especially since losing this hand would result in a devastating loss to the cumulative score.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
