ard of 6d, 7c, Qs, where the opponent has bet 287 chips after a checked pre-flop round. You hold 10s and 9s, giving you only 10-high and a gutshot straight draw (needing an 8 to complete a straight). You have 4 outs (four 8s) to improve, which is roughly an 8.5% chance on the turn. The opponent's bet is large relative to the likely small pot, offering poor pot odds for such a weak draw. Calling would be a negative expected value play.\n\nReason: The probability of completing the straight is too low (approx 9%) to justify calling a large bet. Folding preserves chips for better opportunities.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
